            0.108
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5.4.2 Sublimation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.225,
            0.117,
            0.504,
            0.136
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Activity 5.7: Let us explore"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.235,
            0.138,
            0.89,
            0.19
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "1. Take a spatula full of the mixture containing crushed camphor and sand. Put it into a clean and dry china dish. Place it on the tripod stand using a wire gauze."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.041,
            0.194,
            0.437,
            0.466
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 3,
              "bbox": [
                0.041,
                0.194,
                0.437,
                0.466
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_3.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 4,
              "bbox": [
                0.119,
                0.473,
                0.365,
                0.489
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 5.17: Sublimation of camphor"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.454,
            0.192,
            0.889,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2. Take a clean and dry glass funnel. Plug its nozzle with cotton."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.23,
            0.889,
            0.266
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "3. Keep this funnel inverted on the china dish and set up the apparatus as shown in Fig. 5.17."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.268,
            0.888,
            0.302
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "4. Light the burner and place it under the wire gauze."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.305,
            0.887,
            0.323
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "5. Heat the china dish gently for a few minutes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.325,
            0.888,
            0.361
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6. Observe the inner wall of the funnel carefully. Do you notice any solid deposits?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.455,
            0.363,
            0.89,
            0.415
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. You may find white, solid camphor deposits on the inner wall of the funnel while sand remains in the china dish."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.441,
            0.417,
            0.89,
            0.502
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Camphor, on heating (below its melting point), changes directly from the solid state to the vapour state without passing through the liquid state, this process is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "sublimation",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". On cooling, vapours condense back into a solid without becoming a"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.502,
            0.89,
            0.537
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "liquid, this is called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "deposition",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Hence, camphor sublimes and separates from sand because sand does not sublime on heating."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.539,
            0.889,
            0.574
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "You can also perform this activity using any other sublimable substance, such as naphthalene, in place of camphor."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.223,
            0.576,
            0.89,
            0.628
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Similarly, solid carbon dioxide, known as dry ice which is used for ice cream storage, also undergoes sublimation. Can you think of any other mixtures where sublimation can be used to separate the components?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.253,
            0.631,
            0.67,
            0.649
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Is it possible to dissolve one metal in another?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.222,
            0.652,
            0.89,
            0.755
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Metals do not dissolve into each other at room temperature. However, when metals are melted at high temperatures, they can be mixed to form a solution. When such a hot mixture of two or more metals cools, it solidifies into a new material that appears to be a single metal. A homogeneous mixture of two or more metals, or a metal and a non-metal, is called an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "alloy",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": ". Physical methods cannot separate the components of an alloy. Alloys"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.042,
            0.759,
            0.101,
            0.789
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.042,
                0.759,
                0.101,
                0.789
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.105,
            0.763,
            0.295,
            0.782
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.067,
            0.794,
            0.312,
            0.841
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Why do immiscible liquids form two separate layers in a separating funnel?"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.066,
            0.84,
            0.31,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "8. Is sublimation different from evaporation? Justify."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.332,
            0.753,
            0.891,
            0.889
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "are generally prepared to produce materials that are stronger, more rigid, or more corrosion-resistant. Common examples of alloys include brass — a mixture of approximately 80% copper and 20% zinc; bronze — a mixture of approximately 80% copper and 20% tin; stainless steel — usually a mixture of iron and other elements, such as carbon (about 0.03 – 0.8%), chromium (16 – 18%), nickel (10.0 –14.0%) and molybdenum (2.0 – 3.0%)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.079,
            0.943,
            0.109,
            0.96
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "84"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
